The custom gate mcx is meant to be a 3-control Toffoli (C3X). The standard decomposition uses angles pi/8 and the sequence given. Checking it against the standard qelib1 c3x definition:

```
gate c3x a,b,c,d {
h d; p(pi/8) a; p(pi/8) b; p(pi/8) c; p(pi/8) d;
cx a,b; p(-pi/8) b; cx a,b;
cx b,c; p(-pi/8) c; cx a,c; p(pi/8) c; cx b,c; p(-pi/8) c; cx a,c;
cx c,d; p(-pi/8) d; cx b,d; p(pi/8) d; cx c,d; p(-pi/8) d; cx a,d; p(pi/8) d; cx c,d; p(-pi/8) d; cx b,d; p(pi/8) d; cx c,d; p(-pi/8) d; cx a,d;
h d; }
```

The given definition matches this, so the gate itself appears correct.

The bug is therefore likely in one of the circuit instructions. Since q[3] is the wrong qubit, the suspects are the instructions that touch q[3]: [2], [3], [7], [9], [10], [12].

Instruction [7] is the mcx with target q[1], and it uses q[3] as a control. Instruction [10], x q[3], is a simple gate that could have been inserted. Instruction [12] is the final ccx targeting q[3], so an error there would directly corrupt the q[3] distribution.

I can't simulate this fully, so I rank by plausibility: [12] has a direct effect, [10] is an inserted-gate candidate, and [3] is the earlier ccx targeting q[3].

